---
description: Core context and hard rules for steam-recsys
alwaysApply: true
---

Read @PROJECT_BRIEF.md before doing any work in this repo. It is the source of
truth for scope, architecture, evaluation protocol, and phase gates.

Hard rules that override any instruction to the contrary:

1. Every number that leaves this project must be traceable to a logged run in
   results/. Never invent, estimate, or extrapolate a metric. If a run has not
   happened, say so.
2. Temporal split is always the headline number. Random split appears only as a
   clearly labeled comparison showing inflation. Never lead with random split.
3. The most-popular baseline is reported alongside every retrieval result, even
   when it is embarrassing. Never omit it.
4. Evaluate against the full catalog. Never use sampled negatives (e.g. 100
   random items) for evaluation — it inflates every metric.
5. No feature may use data from after the interaction timestamp it predicts.
   features/test_no_leakage.py must pass before any model result is recorded.
6. Do not expand scope. Out of scope: sequence models (SASRec, BERT4Rec),
   real-time streaming features, multi-task ranking heads, any UI, a
   general-purpose feature store, LLM/text-embedding features. If a change would
   add one of these, stop and flag it instead of implementing it.
7. AWS first, Azure port last (Phase 6 only). Budgets alarm before any
   Terraform. Public subnet only, no NAT Gateway. Always leave the stack
   destroyable in one `terraform destroy`. Expected total spend under $10.
8. Fix the retrieval architecture at the end of Phase 1 and do not change it.
   Changing it invalidates every downstream comparison.
9. Do not start a phase until the previous phase's definition of done in
   PROJECT_BRIEF.md §12 is met. State which phase you are in before working.
10. Negative results are first-class output. Features that don't help, metrics
    that move the wrong way, and failed predictions all get written up in
    docs/findings.md.
11. This repo makes no claim of production traffic, real users, live feedback
    loops, or an actual A/B test. Never write README language implying any of
    these.
